module

public import Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Limits.Shapes.Pullback.ChosenPullback
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Descent.QuasiCoherent.StandardAffineSeparated
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Descent.QuasiCoherent.ZariskiStack

/-!
# Reducing quasi-coherent descent along a family to descent along a single morphism

Stacks 023S considers a standard fpqc covering `{Spec Aₖ ⟶ T}` of an affine scheme, and its proof
opens with the sentence *"hence we may assume `n = 1`"*: the family is replaced by the single
morphism `Spec (∏ₖ Aₖ) ⟶ T`.  This file carries out that replacement.

The step is **not** a sieve refinement.  The sieve generated by `{Spec Aₖ ⟶ T}` is contained in
the sieve generated by `Spec (∏ₖ Aₖ) ⟶ T`, and the containment is strict, while Mathlib's
refinement lemmas
(`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.isEquivalence_toDescentData_of_sieve_le`,
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.IsStackFor.of_le`) transport descent from the *smaller* sieve to the
larger one.  What does replace it is the observation that the family is a **Zariski open covering
of the intermediate scheme**: `Spec Aₖ ⟶ Spec (∏ₖ Aₖ)` are open immersions with covering images
(`Stacks.Descent.iSup_opensRange_specMap_piComponent`).  Everything here is therefore proved in
that generality — for an arbitrary `b : Z ⟶ S` and an arbitrary Zariski open covering `{u k}` of
`Z` with `u k ≫ b = g k` — and the standard affine covering is the special case
`Z = Spec (∏ₖ Aₖ)`.  In particular the *disjointness* of the pieces of the clopen decomposition of
`Spec (∏ₖ Aₖ)` plays no role: the transition data required by the Zariski descent datum on `Z` is
supplied by the descent datum for the family itself, through
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctor`.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.Descent.familyPullFunctor`: the comparison functor from descent data for the single
  morphism `b : Z ⟶ S` to descent data for the family `g`, namely
  `CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctor` at `p = 𝟙 S` and `p' = u`.
* `Stacks.Descent.restrictFamilyFunctor`: the functor from descent data for `g` to descent data for
  the Zariski covering `u` of `Z`, namely the same `pullFunctor` at `p = b` and `p' = 𝟙`.  It is
  what turns a descent datum for the family into a *Zariski* descent datum on `Z`.
* `Stacks.Descent.pairwisePreimageOpen`, `Stacks.Descent.pairwisePreimageFactor₁` and
  `Stacks.Descent.pairwisePreimageFactor₂`: for two morphisms `f₁ f₂ : Y ⟶ Z`, the open covering of
  `Y` by the `f₁ ⁻¹ᵁ (u k).opensRange ⊓ f₂ ⁻¹ᵁ (u l).opensRange` together with the factorisations
  of `f₁` and `f₂` through `u k` and `u l` on each member.
* `Stacks.Descent.triplePreimageOpen` and `Stacks.Descent.triplePreimageFactor₁`,
  `Stacks.Descent.triplePreimageFactor₂`, `Stacks.Descent.triplePreimageFactor₃`: the same for
  three morphisms `f₁ f₂ f₃ : Y ⟶ Z`.
* `Stacks.Descent.chosenPullbackOfHasPullback` and
  `Stacks.Descent.chosenPullback₃OfHasPullback`: `CategoryTheory.Limits.ChosenPullback` and
  `CategoryTheory.Limits.ChosenPullback₃` from the mere existence of pullbacks, which is how the
  chosen pullbacks that `CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData'` consumes are produced for
  schemes.
* `Stacks.Descent.transitionIso`: **the transition isomorphism over `Z ×[S] Z`** attached to a
  descent datum for the family and a quasi-coherent module `N` on `Z` restricting to it.  See the
  implementation notes for the route, which avoids every explicit gluing.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Descent.sieve_ofArrows_le_sieve_ofArrows_singleton`: the sieve generated by the family is
  contained in the sieve generated by the single morphism, which is the inequality in the direction
  Mathlib's refinement lemmas cannot use.
* `Stacks.Descent.faithful_familyPullFunctor`: `Stacks.Descent.familyPullFunctor` is faithful,
  by the Zariski separatedness `Stacks.Descent.hom_ext_of_pullback_map_eq` on `Z`.
* `Stacks.Descent.exists_iso_toDescentData_restrictFamilyFunctor`: the **object half** of the
  reduction.  The components of a descent datum for the family glue to a quasi-coherent module on
  `Z`, by the Zariski stack condition `Stacks.Descent.isStack_qcohPseudofunctor`.
* `Stacks.Descent.essSurj_qcohToDescentData_of_essSurj_familyPullFunctor` and
  `Stacks.Descent.isEquivalence_qcohToDescentData_of_essSurj_familyPullFunctor`: the reduction
  itself.  Descent along `b` plus essential surjectivity of `Stacks.Descent.familyPullFunctor`
  gives descent along the family.
* `Stacks.Descent.essSurj_qcohToDescentData_specMap_of_essSurj_familyPullFunctor` and
  `Stacks.Descent.isEquivalence_qcohToDescentData_specMap_of_essSurj_familyPullFunctor`: the
  specialisation to a family of ring maps `ρ k : R ⟶ A k`, which is the shape of Stacks 023S;
  `Stacks.Descent.faithful_familyPullFunctor_specMap` and
  `Stacks.Descent.exists_iso_toDescentData_restrictFamilyFunctor_specMap` are the faithfulness and
  object halves there, which do use finiteness of the index type.
* `Stacks.Descent.qcoh_hom_ext_of_map_eq`: Zariski separatedness of morphisms read inside
  `Stacks.Descent.qcohPseudofunctor`, which is the form in which the gluing arguments use it.
* `Stacks.Descent.fullyFaithfulFamilyPullFunctor`: `Stacks.Descent.familyPullFunctor` is **fully
  faithful** as soon as the family is covering for a topology for which quasi-coherent modules
  form a prestack.  It is literally the functor Mathlib's
  `CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.fullyFaithfulPullFunctor` applies to, with the
  factorisation hypothesis `hg` accepted verbatim as that lemma's commutative square.
* `Stacks.Descent.exists_map_eq_of_pullHom_eq`: **gluing of morphisms**, for an arbitrary
  pseudofunctor whose descent functor for the family is full.  A family of morphisms between the
  pullbacks of two objects which agree after any further pullback comes from a morphism of the
  objects themselves.  This is Mathlib's
  `CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.subtypeCompatibleHomEquiv` read through fullness, and
  for `Stacks.Descent.qcohPseudofunctor` and a Zariski covering family the fullness hypothesis is
  `Stacks.Descent.nonempty_fullyFaithful_qcohToDescentData`.

## Implementation notes

`Stacks.Descent.familyPullFunctor` is given `p = 𝟙 S` explicitly rather than letting it be
inferred, so that it is the functor to which Mathlib's
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.fullyFaithfulPullFunctor` applies verbatim.

The faithfulness proof names the pullback of the equation with a `have` before using it.  Writing
`exact congrArg InducedCategory.Hom.hom h` instead makes the elaborator reconstruct the subcategory
morphism from the expected type and it fails with an application type mismatch; naming the term
leaves it to be checked at default transparency.

`Stacks.Descent.specMap_piComponent_comp` exists so that the factorisation hypothesis `hg` has one
spelling at every call site in the affine specialisations; two different proofs of it are equal,
but crossing between them inside the dependent types of this area is expensive.

**The route to `Stacks.Descent.transitionIso` uses no gluing at all.**  Write `W` for the chosen
pullback of `b` with itself and `V kl` for the members of the covering
`Stacks.Descent.pairwisePreimageOpen u sqb.p₁ sqb.p₂` of `W`.  The two legs
`Stacks.Descent.transitionFactor₁` and `Stacks.Descent.transitionFactor₂` make the family
`V kl ⟶ W` map to the family `g` over `S` in two ways with the *same* morphism `W ⟶ S`, so
Mathlib's `CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctorIso` — the statement that
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctor` depends on its auxiliary data only up to a
canonical isomorphism — already supplies an isomorphism between the two induced functors
`qcohPseudofunctor.DescentData g ⥤ qcohPseudofunctor.DescentData (fun kl ↦ (V kl).ι)`.  Its
components are exactly the transition morphisms `D.hom _ (transitionFactor₁ _ _ kl)
(transitionFactor₂ _ _ kl)` of the given descent datum, so the compatibility that a hand-rolled
gluing would have to prove is Mathlib's, not ours.  `Stacks.Descent.transitionGluedIso` then
composes that isomorphism with `CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctorCompIso` and
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.toDescentDataCompPullFunctorIso` on both sides, and
`Stacks.Descent.transitionIso` is its preimage under the fully faithful descent functor of the
Zariski covering `(V kl).ι` of `W`.

Three cost facts govern how that is written:

* `Stacks.Descent.transitionCompIso₁`, `Stacks.Descent.transitionCompIso₂`,
  `Stacks.Descent.transitionBaseIso₁` and `Stacks.Descent.transitionBaseIso₂` carry **no type
  ascription**.  Ascribing the mathematically correct type — `restrictFamilyFunctor b u g hg ⋙ Θ₁ ≅
  Φ₁` for named `Θ₁` and `Φ₁` — makes the elaborator unify two `def`-wrapped
  `CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctor`s and it dies at the heartbeat limit in
  15 s.  With all arguments supplied explicitly and the type inferred the same term elaborates in
  2 s.
* `Stacks.Descent.transitionW₁'` and `Stacks.Descent.transitionW₂'` restate
  `Stacks.Descent.transitionW₁` and `Stacks.Descent.transitionW₂` with the index map spelled
  `(fun k ↦ k) ∘ (fun kl ↦ kl.1)` rather than `fun kl ↦ kl.1`, because that is the spelling
  `CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctorCompIso` produces for the composite index
  map; the two are definitionally equal, and the restatement is a term proof so that the crossing
  is checked at default transparency.  A `rw` chain against the composed spelling fails outright.
* `Stacks.Descent.transitionIso` is an `CategoryTheory.Iso`, never an
  `CategoryTheory.IsIso` hypothesis on a morphism.

`Stacks.Descent.chosenPullbackOfHasPullback` and `Stacks.Descent.chosenPullback₃OfHasPullback` are
statements about an arbitrary category; Mathlib has no constructor of
`CategoryTheory.Limits.ChosenPullback` from `CategoryTheory.Limits.HasPullback`.  Likewise
`Stacks.Descent.compatible_presheafHom` and `Stacks.Descent.exists_map_eq_of_pullHom_eq` are
statements about an arbitrary pseudofunctor.

**Every step of the local normal form is proved for an abstract pseudofunctor `F` and only then
instantiated at `Stacks.Descent.qcohPseudofunctor`**, and this is a hard requirement, not a
stylistic preference.  The normal form has to cross between the
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData` world, where a component is
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.Hom.hom`, and the `CategoryTheory.Cat` world, where it
is `CategoryTheory.Cat.Hom₂.toNatTrans` applied at an object.  The two spellings are
definitionally equal, and with `F` a variable the kernel checks that in milliseconds; with
`F := Stacks.Descent.qcohPseudofunctor` the very same check costs over a minute and dies at the
kernel's deterministic timeout — already on the single equation
`((toDescentDataCompPullFunctorIso F w).app N).inv.hom kl =
(F.isoMapOfCommSq (CommSq.mk (w kl)).op.toLoc).hom.toNatTrans.app N`, which is `rfl` either way.
So `Stacks.Descent.mapComp'_hom_app_comp_tdcpfi_app_inv_hom`,
`Stacks.Descent.tdcpfi_app_hom_hom_comp_mapComp'_inv_app` and
`Stacks.Descent.pullHom_eq_of_map_eq` are stated over an arbitrary `F`, and the whole sevenfold
normalisation happens inside the last of them;
`Stacks.Descent.pullHom_transitionIso_hom` is a single application of it.

The same crossing defeats `rw` and `simp` for a different reason: the target and the source of
consecutive factors of `Stacks.Descent.transitionGluedIso` are spelled differently — `(pullFunctor
F w ⋙ pullFunctor F w').obj D` against `(pullFunctor F w).obj ((pullFunctor F w').obj D)` — so
rewriting with `CategoryTheory.Iso.trans_hom` reports that the target is not type-correct under
implicit transparency.  `Stacks.Descent.trans7_hom_hom` and `Stacks.Descent.comp7_conj` exist so
that the decomposition is an *application* instead, checked at default transparency; likewise
`Stacks.Descent.pullHom_comp3` and `Stacks.Descent.map_comp_eq_of_pullHom_comp_eq` take the
factor identities as hypotheses rather than being reached by rewriting.

For the same reason, a `rw` with `Stacks.Descent.pullHom_pullHom_eq` must pin the implicit
`M₁`, `M₂`, `f₁`, `f₂`: left as metavariables they cannot be solved from the type of a component
of `eN` without unfolding `Stacks.Descent.restrictFamilyFunctor`, and the rewrite then fails to
find its own pattern.  The same holds for the strict-implicit indices of
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData'.pullHom'`, which are pinned as `(i₁ := ())
(i₂ := ())` throughout.

